WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 313: Lady Jane Grey: The Nine Days Queen, the Traitor's Gate, and the Innocent Martyr

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.040
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series chronicles one of the most heartbreaking figures in royal history.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:21.600
[Rebecca] A brilliant teenage scholar crowned against her will, who ruled for just nine days before meeting her fate upon the scaffold, Lady Jane Grey.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:28.000
[Rebecca] Here is George with the tragic story of the Nine Days Queen.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:41.120
[George] Born in October 1537 at Bradgate Park in Leicestershire, Lady Jane Grey was the eldest daughter of Henry Grey, Marquess of Dorset, and Lady Frances Brandon, niece to King Henry VIII.

7
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.920
[George] From infancy, Jane was raised in an atmosphere of aristocratic severity.

8
00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:56.160
[George] Her parents, demanding absolute physical perfection and courtly ambition, subjected the young girl to harsh scoldings, pinches, and blows for the slightest infraction.

9
00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:02.160
[George] Seeking sanctuary from emotional abuse, Jane immersed herself in the world of classical books.

10
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:09.200
[George] Tutored by the reformist scholar John Eilmer, Jane displayed intellectual brilliance that stunned Renaissance Europe.

11
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.960
[George] By age 13, she was corresponding and fluent.

12
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:21.360
[George] Elegant Latin and Greek with the Swiss Protestant theologian Heinrich Bullinger, reading Hebrew and translating Italian poetry.

13
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:35.680
[George] In the summer of 1550, when the famous scholar Roger Asham visited Bradgate, he found the entire household hunting deer in the park while 14-year-old Jane sat alone in her chamber, reading Plato's Fido in original Greek.

14
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:48.720
[George] When Asham asked why she abandoned the hunt, Jane famously smiled and replied that all the sport in the park was but a shadow to the pleasure she found in Plato, explaining that in her books she found a gentle paradise.

15
00:01:48.720 --> 00:02:01.880
[George] Jane's love for books was nurtured by her close relationship with Catherine Parr, Henry VIII's surviving sixth wife, in whose enlightened household at Chelsea Manor Jane resided following the old king's death.

16
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:11.000
[George] Catherine introduced Jane to the most progressive reformist minds in England, creating an environment where a young noblewoman could debate theology as an equal.

17
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.600
[George] Her letters to Bullinger in Zurich reveal a mind of astonishing theological sophistication.

18
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:29.320
[George] She analyzed the Greek New Testament, debated the nature of grace, and expressed profound contempt for courtly vanities like gold embroidery and braided hair, preferring the quiet contemplation of scripture.

19
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:35.800
[George] Jane's royal blood made her a dangerous pawn in the high-stakes chess match of Tudor dynastic succession.

20
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.200
[George] When fifteen-year-old King Edward V.

21
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:51.160
[George] I fell terminally ill with tuberculosis in early 1553, John Dudley, Duke of Northumberland, hatched an audacious scheme to keep the crown in Protestant hands and secure his own family's supremacy.

22
00:02:51.160 --> 00:03:01.640
[George] In May 1553, Jane was coerced by her parents into a political marriage with Northumberland's handsome, arrogant younger son, Lord Guildford Dudley.

23
00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:10.120
[George] When Jane fiercely refused the match, preferring a life of scholarly retreat, her father beat her savagely until she tearfully submitted.

24
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.680
[George] The wedding took place in London amid dazzling pageantry, with Jane dressed in cloth of gold and pearls.

25
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:26.920
[George] Concurrently, King Edward drafted his devise for the succession, bypassing his Catholic half-sister Mary and Elizabeth to name Jane and her heir's successors to the throne.

26
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:33.400
[George] When Edward died on July 6, Northumberland concealed the death for three days to consolidate power.

27
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:43.880
[George] On July 9, Jane was summoned to Sea Unhouse on the Thames, where Northumberland and the royal council fell on their knees before her, proclaiming her sovereign queen of England.

28
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:53.520
[George] Jane fainted in shock, weeping that she was utterly insufficient, but praying that if God had laid the burden upon her, he would grant her grace to govern.

29
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.240
[George] Northumberland's dynastic conspiracy was motivated by sheer survival.

30
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.640
[George] He knew that if Catholic Mary ascended the throne, his neck would be the first beneath the executioner's axe.

31
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:13.280
[George] When Jane was brought to sea unhouse, the assembled lords dropped to their knees on the rush matting, with her own parents bowing low before her.

32
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:28.160
[George] Jane collapsed to the floor in genuine, inconsolable grief, weeping that she knew Edward's death meant disaster for the realm, stating that if the crown belonged to her by right, she prayed God would give her grace to govern it to his glory and service.

33
00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:42.640
[George] On July 10, 1553, sixteen-year-old Jane made her solemn royal entry into the Tower of London by State Barge, dressed in a royal gown of green velvet embroidered with gold, her long train carried by her mother.

34
00:04:42.640 --> 00:05:02.080
[George] But the citizens of London received their new queen with stony, sullen silence, a contemporary witness recorded that not a single voice shouted God save Queen Jane, except for one teenage apprentice boy who loudly declared Mary was the rightful heir, for which Northumberland's guards nailed his ears to a pillory.

35
00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.120
[George] Inside the royal apartments of the tower, Jane asserted her moral independence.

36
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:14.880
[George] When the Marquess of Winchester brought her the royal crown to try on for size, Jane hesitated, stating she had never sought it.

37
00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.480
[George] Winchester told her that another crown would be fashioned to crown her husband Guildford as king.

38
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.200
[George] Jane flared with royal indignation.

39
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:31.720
[George] She refused to make Guildford king, stating she was willing to make him a duke, but would not surrender the sovereign Majesty of England to her husband.

40
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:42.200
[George] Furious marital arguments erupted, with Guildford storming from the room and his mother cursing Jane, revealing the dysfunctional rot at the core of the Dudley regime.

41
00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:49.800
[George] During her nine days as Queen, Jane displayed an unbending moral integrity that infuriated her ambitious father-in-law.

42
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:58.760
[George] When Northumberland demanded that she sign commissions appointing his sons to high military commands, Jane insisted on reviewing every document personally.

43
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:09.800
[George] She refused to allow the royal treasury to be plundered for private bribes, and when the Duchess of Northumberland threatened to remove Guildford from the tower unless Jane consented to make him king.

44
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:18.280
[George] Jane coldly replied that she would not be intimidated in her own council chamber, proving that the teenage scholar was no pliable puppet.

45
00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:25.800
[George] While Northumberland had military control of London, he fatally underestimated Princess Mary's courage and popularity.

46
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:34.840
[George] Evading Dudley's cavalry traps, Mary fled into the fastnesses of East Anglia, establishing her headquarters at Framlingham Castle in Suffolk.

47
00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:47.160
[George] Rallying Catholic gentry, Protestant yeomen, and royalist nobles alike who revered the legitimate bloodline of Henry Vii, Mary raised a loyal army of thirty thousand men within days.

48
00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:55.160
[George] When Northumberland marched out of London with an army to confront Mary, his troops melted away, defecting to the rightful princess.

49
00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.680
[George] Left behind in London, the royal council panicked.

50
00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.520
[George] On July 19, recognizing that the game was lost.

51
00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:10.840
[George] The Earl of Pembroke and the councillors gathered at Cheapside Cross and proclaimed Mary Queen of England amid wild, ecstatic rejoicing.

52
00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:18.240
[George] Church bells pealed across the city, bonfires were lit on every street corner, and citizens danced in the lanes.

53
00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:30.000
[George] Jane's father, the Duke of Suffolk, personally entered Jane's council chamber in the tower, tore down the cloth of state above her chair with his own hands, and told her she was no longer Queen.

54
00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.160
[George] Jane replied with quiet relief, Can I go home now?

55
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.960
[George] But the doors of the tower locked behind her, the palace was now her prison.

56
00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.480
[George] The collapse of Jane's regime was so swift that her royal reign vanished like a summer mist.

57
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:56.000
[George] When the Duke of Suffolk tore down the cloth of estate in the council chamber, Jane asked with heartbreaking childlike innocence whether she might leave the fortress and return to her quiet books at Bradgate.

58
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:04.160
[George] Suffolk, terrified for his own life, fled the tower, leaving his daughter and son-in-law to be locked in separate prison lodgings.

59
00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:17.280
[George] That evening, as bonfires blazed along the Thames and church bells rang across London in celebration of Queen Mary, Jane sat in her stone chamber, abandoned by all who had placed the heavy crown upon her brow.

60
00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.400
[George] Jane and Guildford were separated and placed under armed guard within the Tower Fortress.

61
00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:32.960
[George] Guildford in the Beauchamp Tower, where he carved the name Iane into the cold stone wall, and Jane in the gentleman jailer's lodgings on Tower Green overlooking the scaffold.

62
00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:45.520
[George] Queen Mary, initially inclined toward royal clemency, recognized that Jane was an innocent pawn who had acted under duress, and resisted imperial demands from the Spanish ambassador to execute the girl.

63
00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.560
[George] But the formalities of law had to be observed.

64
00:08:48.560 --> 00:09:03.000
[George] On November 13, 1553, Jane, dressed in a simple black woolen gown and carrying a prayer book, was walked through the streets of London to Guildhall to stand trial for high treason alongside Cranmer and the Dudley brothers.

65
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:09.560
[George] When the indictment was read, Jane calmly pleaded guilty, acknowledging that she had accepted the crown.

66
00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:17.080
[George] The judge pronounced the terrifying medieval sentence to be burned alive on Tower Hill or beheaded at the Queen's pleasure.

67
00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:22.520
[George] Jane listened without flinching, her composure moving hardened spectators to tears.

68
00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:30.600
[George] Returned to the tower, she spent her days writing theological letters and meditating upon scripture, preparing her soul for eternity.

69
00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:36.920
[George] During her long months in the gentleman jailer's lodgings, Jane found spiritual solace in writing.

70
00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:46.520
[George] Using a needle and silk thread, she embroidered a small prayer book for her sister Catherine, and with a pin scratched Latin verses into the wooden wall of her cell.

71
00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:51.240
[George] To think that we are innocent is vain, yet let us suffer with patience.

72
00:09:51.240 --> 00:10:02.200
[George] Her composure at her guildhall trial was described by eyewitnesses as angelic, surrounded by halberdiers carrying the ceremonial axe with its blade turned toward her after conviction.

73
00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:10.040
[George] Jane walked back through the crowded London streets with head held high, consoling her weeping gentlewomen instead of seeking pity.

74
00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.560
[George] Jane's fate was sealed not by her own actions, but by the reckless folly of others.

75
00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:27.320
[George] In late 1553, Queen Mary announced her intended marriage to Prince Philip of Spain, igniting widespread nationalist fury across England at the prospect of foreign Spanish domination.

76
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:36.920
[George] In January 1554, Sir Thomas Wyatt the Younger launched a massive armed rebellion in Kent, marching four thousand armed men on London.

77
00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:46.640
[George] Incredibly, Jane's foolish father, the Duke of Suffolk, joined the rebellion, riding through the Midlands attempting to raise troops for his daughter once again.

78
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:51.520
[George] Wyatt's rebels breached the outskirts of London before being crushed at Ludgate Hill.

79
00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.360
[George] The Spanish ambassadors delivered an ultimatum to Queen Mary.

80
00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:02.720
[George] Prince Philip would not set foot on English soil so long as Lady Jane Grey lived as a figurehead for Protestant insurrection.

81
00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.520
[George] Mary reluctantly signed the death warrants.

82
00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:13.920
[George] Desperate to save Jane's soul, Mary sent her personal confessor, Abbot John Feckenham, to convert Jane to Catholicism.

83
00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:25.680
[George] In a remarkable three-day theological debate in Jane's prison cell, the teenage girl matched the learned abbot's scripture for scripture, respectfully defending justification by faith alone.

84
00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:30.960
[George] Feckenham wept as he parted from her, saying he was sorry they would not meet in heaven.

85
00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.480
[George] To which Jane replied, I trust we shall, for God hath showed me mercy.

86
00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:46.880
[George] Abbot Feckenam's theological debates with Jane in her prison cell were transcribed and published across Europe, becoming a foundational text of the English Protestant martyrology.

87
00:11:46.880 --> 00:12:00.880
[George] When Feckenam argued that good works were essential for salvation, Jane responded with brilliant scholastic precision, arguing that faith alone justifies, but that true faith produces good works as a living tree bears fruit.

88
00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:13.600
[George] Fecenham was so moved by her intellectual brilliance and gentle sincerity that he personally petitioned Queen Mary for a three-day delay of execution, accompanying the young woman to the scaffold to pray for her soul.

89
00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:23.920
[George] On the freezing morning of February 12, 1554, Jane watched from her window as her young husband Guildford Dudley was led out to Tower Hill to be beheaded.

90
00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:35.320
[George] Minutes later, she witnessed the horse cart returning into the fortress carrying his severed head and blood-soaked corpse wrapped in a sheet, whispering in tears, O Guildford, Guildford.

91
00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:37.000
[George] The bitter cup of death.

92
00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:41.960
[George] At ten o'clock, Jane was escorted to the private scaffold on Tower Green.

93
00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:47.560
[George] Clad in black, she mounted the wooden stairs with steady steps and addressed the onlookers.

94
00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:52.360
[George] Good people, I am come hither to die, and by law I am condemned to the same.

95
00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:59.080
[George] The fact indeed against the Queen's Highness was grievous, but the procurement thereof by me was never of my will.

96
00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:01.800
[George] I wash my hands thereof in innocency.

97
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:09.160
[George] Handing her gloves and handkerchief to her weeping gentlewoman, the executioner tied a white linen blindfold over her eyes.

98
00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:17.320
[George] Reaching out with trembling hands, she could not find the wooden block in the darkness, crying out in panicked, childlike vulnerability.

99
00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:18.680
[George] What shall I do?

100
00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:19.720
[George] Where is it?

101
00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:20.760
[George] Where is it?

102
00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:23.560
[George] A bystander guided her hands to the block.

103
00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:32.440
[George] Kneeling, she laid her head down, murmured her final words, Lord, into thy hands I commend my spirit, and the heavy broadax fell.

104
00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:37.080
[George] She was sixteen years old, buried beneath the altar of the chapel of St.

105
00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:45.240
[George] Peter ad Vincula beside two queens, Jane Grey passed into immortality as the innocent flower of the English Reformation.

106
00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:49.320
[George] The tragedy of Jane's death sent shockwaves through Tudor England.

107
00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:58.440
[George] The executioner knelt before her on the scaffold, begging for her forgiveness, which she freely granted, asking only that he dispatch her quickly.

108
00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:05.640
[George] Her momentary, terrifying panic when blindfolded, groping blindly through the dark air crying, Where is it?

109
00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:11.720
[George] etched itself into the national memory as the supreme symbol of innocence crushed by courtly ambition.

110
00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:26.240
[George] Within days of her execution, the judge who condemned her, Sir Richard Morgan, went violently insane, screaming in his bed that the Lady Jane was haunting him, an ominous warning to the new regime of the blood guilt it had incurred.

111
00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.560
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

112
00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:46.000
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of Lady Jane Grey, the Nine Days Queen, the Traitor's Gate, and The Innocent Martyr, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

113
00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.640
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

